{
 "id": "l03",
 "label": "3",
 "title": "Greetings and common expressions",
 "summary": "Po and opo, greetings through the day, how are you, excuse me, and everyday phrases",
 "minutes": 30,
 "goals": [
  "Use po, ho, opo and oho to show respect, and know when to leave them out",
  "Greet people at every time of day, politely and casually",
  "Ask and answer \"how are you?\" and recognise friendly question-greetings",
  "Choose the right way to say excuse me, sorry or pardon",
  "Use a set of everyday expressions"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Po and ho: speaking respectfully",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Respect is built into Tagalog. The little words **po** and **ho** are added to a sentence to show courtesy, much like \"sir\" or \"madam\" in English. They have no other meaning."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Who is speaking to whom",
      "Use po or ho?"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "A younger person to an older person",
       "Yes"
      ],
      [
       "A junior colleague to a senior one",
       "Yes"
      ],
      [
       "Anyone to a teacher, a priest, an official, an employer or a police officer, whatever their age",
       "Yes"
      ],
      [
       "Anyone to someone they have just met and want to be polite to",
       "Yes"
      ],
      [
       "An older person to a younger one (a parent to a child)",
       "No"
      ],
      [
       "Equals: brothers, sisters and close friends",
       "No (it is not needed)"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "When to use po"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Yes** and **no** show the same pattern. Between friends, **oo** means yes. To an older person, say **opo** or **oho**, the polite forms of yes. For no, add po: **hindi po**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Casual",
      "Polite",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[oo|part]]",
       "[[opo|part]] or [[oho|part]]",
       "yes"
      ],
      [
       "[[hindi|part]]",
       "[[hindi po|part]]",
       "no"
      ],
      [
       "[[Salamat.|part]]",
       "[[Salamat po.|part]]",
       "Thank you."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Casual and polite"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "Where po goes",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Short answer",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Opo",
         "r": "part"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Added to a sentence",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Salamat",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "po",
         "r": "part"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "Po usually follows the first word or the main phrase of a sentence."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "culture",
     "title": "Good manners",
     "t": "Using po too often is never wrong. Foreigners and Filipinos alike will not go wrong with extra politeness, especially to older people."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Greetings through the day",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Most greetings start with **magandang**, which means \"beautiful\" (the linker -ng joins it to the next word). So **Magandang araw** is literally \"beautiful day\". Then you add the part of the day."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Greeting",
      "Meaning",
      "Time"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[Magandang araw|pred]]",
       "Good day",
       "any time in daylight"
      ],
      [
       "[[Magandang umaga|pred]]",
       "Good morning",
       "morning"
      ],
      [
       "[[Magandang tanghali|pred]]",
       "Good midday",
       "roughly 11 am to 1 pm"
      ],
      [
       "[[Magandang hapon|pred]]",
       "Good afternoon",
       "afternoon"
      ],
      [
       "[[Magandang gabi|pred]]",
       "Good evening, good night",
       "evening and night"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Greetings by time"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "A polite greeting",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Parts",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Magandang",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "umaga",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "po",
         "r": "part"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "Between equals you can drop po: Magandang umaga."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Magandang umaga po.",
       "en": "Good morning, sir or madam.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Magandang",
         "beautiful (with -ng)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "umaga",
         "morning",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "po",
         "polite word",
         "part"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Magandang hapon po.",
       "en": "Good afternoon, sir or madam.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Magandang",
         "beautiful (with -ng)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "hapon",
         "afternoon",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "po",
         "polite word",
         "part"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Magandang gabi.",
       "en": "Good evening (casual).",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Magandang",
         "beautiful (with -ng)",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "gabi",
         "evening",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Tap to hear"
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "Replying"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The usual reply repeats the greeting and adds **rin** (or **din**) or **naman**, which mean \"too\". It shows you wish the same to them."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Greeting",
      "Reply"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Magandang umaga po.",
       "Magandang umaga rin po."
      ],
      [
       "Magandang hapon.",
       "Magandang hapon din."
      ],
      [
       "Magandang gabi po.",
       "Magandang gabi naman po."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Greeting and reply"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "rin or din?",
     "t": "They mean the same. Use **rin** after a word that ends in a vowel (umaga rin) and **din** after a word that ends in a consonant (hapon din)."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "Extra respect",
     "t": "You can add **sa inyo** (\"to you\", respectful) to the end: Magandang umaga po sa inyo. Among equals, **sa iyo** is the casual way to say \"to you\"."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "How are you?",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog **Kumusta** comes from the Spanish *cómo está*. To ask a friend, a relative or someone about your age, say **Kumusta ka?** To show respect, or to ask more than one person, say **Kumusta kayo?** or **Kumusta po kayo?**"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Situation",
      "Say",
      "Pronoun"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "A friend, a child, someone your age",
       "[[Kumusta ka?|pred]]",
       "ka = you (one person, casual)"
      ],
      [
       "An older person, or several people",
       "[[Kumusta po kayo?|pred]]",
       "kayo = you (respectful or plural)"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Asking how someone is"
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Kumusta ka?",
       "en": "How are you?",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kumusta",
         "how are",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ka",
         "you (casual)",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Kumusta po kayo?",
       "en": "How are you, sir or madam?",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Kumusta",
         "how are",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "po",
         "polite word",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "kayo",
         "you (respectful)",
         "pron"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mabuti naman po.",
       "en": "Fine, thank you, sir or madam.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mabuti",
         "fine, good",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "naman",
         "too, in turn",
         "part"
        ],
        [
         "po",
         "polite word",
         "part"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Salamat po.",
       "en": "Thank you, sir or madam.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Salamat",
         "thank you",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
