  {
   "title": "Excuse me, sorry, pardon",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "English uses \"excuse me\" for many situations. Tagalog has **five** expressions, and the right one depends on what you are doing."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Say",
      "Use it when…",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[Paumanhin po|pred]]",
       "you say sorry for a small thing, bump into someone, or leave a meeting",
       "Paumanhin po, aalis na po ako. (Excuse me, I will leave now.)"
      ],
      [
       "[[Pakiraan po|pred]]",
       "you need to pass through a crowded space",
       "Pakiraan po. (Please let me through.)"
      ],
      [
       "[[Mawalang-galang po|pred]]",
       "you politely interrupt, ask a question or ask for attention",
       "Mawalang-galang po, may tanong po ako. (Pardon me, I have a question.)"
      ],
      [
       "[[Patawad po|pred]]",
       "you have truly hurt someone, in body or feeling, and ask forgiveness",
       "Patawad po. (Please forgive me.)"
      ],
      [
       "[[Pasintabi po|pred]]",
       "you warn people before something unpleasant or offensive",
       "Pasintabi po. (With your permission, before I say or show this.)"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Five ways to say excuse me"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Which can replace which?",
     "items": [
      "**Mawalang-galang po** is flexible. It works for getting attention and also for passing through.",
      "**Paumanhin po** is too mild for a real injury or a deep hurt. Use **Patawad po** there.",
      "News readers use **Pasintabi po** before showing something disturbing."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "culture",
     "title": "Why so many?",
     "t": "Courtesy matters in Filipino life. Choosing the right expression shows that you understand the situation and respect the other person."
    }
   ]
  },
